\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2.1}{\ignorespaces Why the width is decided \emph  {at pop time}. Bar $2$ (height $5$) sits on the increasing stack until bar $4$ (height $2$) is lower and pops it. At that instant its left-boundary shorter bar is the new stack top, bar $1$ (height $1$), and its right-boundary shorter bar is $i=4$; the rectangle of height $5$ spans bars $2$--$3$, width $4-1-1=2$, area $10$. That is Theorem~\ref {thm:c26-hist} and the answer for $H$.}}{552}{figure.caption.96}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c26-histfig}{{9.2.1}{552}{Why the width is decided \emph {at pop time}. Bar $2$ (height $5$) sits on the increasing stack until bar $4$ (height $2$) is lower and pops it. At that instant its left-boundary shorter bar is the new stack top, bar $1$ (height $1$), and its right-boundary shorter bar is $i=4$; the rectangle of height $5$ spans bars $2$--$3$, width $4-1-1=2$, area $10$. That is \thmref {c26-hist} and the answer for $H$}{figure.caption.96}{}}
